is. The colors 2 and 5 are not assigned uniformly within these components. The Train 1 example shows a mix. A simple bipartite pattern also fails. I'm moving toward an Eulerian path or more complex block transformation based on 2s and 5s in output.\n\n\n**Discovering Symmetry Pattern**\n\nI've made a breakthrough! I just noticed a vertical symmetry pattern in the placement of the output color 2 across Train 1. It seems that rows 0 and 5, 1 and 4, and 2 and 3 are identical, which is very promising. This might indicate that the transformation is symmetric along the horizontal axis, and the output is likely connected to symmetry. I am checking the 8s on the corresponding positions to see if this explains the transformation.\n\n\n**Confirming Symmetric Transformation**\n\nI've made a huge leap! The pattern is clear: a cell (r, c) becomes 2 if and only if both (r, c) and (5-r, c) are 8s in the input. Rows 0 and 5, 1 and 4, and 2 and 3 exhibit perfect symmetry. I'm verifying this with Train 1, and the intersection of 8s consistently maps to 2. It holds true across all the row pairs!\n\n\n**Verifying Transformation Logic**\n\nI'm now fully immersed in validating my hypothesis across all provided examples! I have tested Train 2 and Train 3, and found that the intersection of 8s across symmetric rows consistently maps to 2, and the other 8's are mapped to 5. The pattern remains remarkably consistent, and I'm very pleased with these results.\n\n\n",
